+The Astro site never transcodes video. It only selects which R2 object the browser should play.
+
+## Large photos: lightweight gallery previews
+
+Remote photos are served by R2 as-is, so a 25 MB PNG used directly in a gallery card would cost 25 MB before the visitor even opens it. Photo entries now support an optional `thumbnail` field:
+
+```yaml
+image: "https://media.b33low.com/photos/Remontee.png"
+thumbnail: "https://media.b33low.com/photos/previews/Remontee.webp"
+```
+
+`thumbnail` is used by the Photography grids/homepage. `image` remains the full-resolution source used when the visitor opens the lightbox or photo page. Existing entries without `thumbnail` keep working and fall back to `image`.
+
+A small helper script publishes both versions through the existing Ant Media utility container:
 
-Optional longer write-up / behind-the-scenes notes go here, rendered as the film's article body.
+```bash
+./scripts/publish-photo.sh ~/Pictures/Remontee.png
 ```
 
-`videoUrl` is played back with a plain `<video>` tag (see `src/components/media/video-player.astro`)
-— no YouTube/Vimeo re-encode, so whatever you upload is exactly what visitors get.
-
-## Media hosting workflow (R2)
-
-1. Shoot, cull/organize privately with something like **Immich** (self-hosted, great for reviewing
-   raw footage — not meant to be your public gallery though).
-2. Export your final cuts/edits at full quality.
-3. Upload the finished files to a **Cloudflare R2** bucket. R2 has no egress fees, which matters
-   once people start actually watching/downloading video.
-4. Put a custom domain in front of the bucket (e.g. `media.hexadecilab.com`) via a Cloudflare
-   Worker or R2's built-in custom domain support, and use that as `MEDIA_BASE_URL` in
-   `src/config.ts` / in each entry's `videoUrl` / `image`.
-5. If you want adaptive-quality streaming instead of a single flat file, look at **PeerTube** —
-   it supports S3-compatible storage (R2) natively and can sit in front of the bucket, and you'd
-   swap the `<video>` tag for an embed of the PeerTube player.
-
-This keeps the "manager" (Immich for private review, optionally PeerTube for public streaming)
-separate from the blog itself — the Astro site just needs a URL.
+It keeps the original unchanged and generates a max-1600px WebP preview at quality 82 before uploading both files to R2.
